    "en":  {
               "headline":  "Nebius down 47% since Q3: delays and capex costs outweigh strong demand",
               "technical":  "NBIS closed at $81.31 on 2026-11-20, down 16.11% in one session. It opened at $93.69 and finished near the day\u0027s low of $80.67. The trend is clearly down. EMAs (exponential moving averages that track short-, medium- and long-term trend) sit far above the price: the EMA50 at $164.9 is about 51% higher, and the EMA100 ($181.1) and EMA200 ($172.04) are higher still. The EMA50 has already fallen below both longer averages, a classic sign that the earlier uptrend has broken. Momentum is negative. MACD (a trend-momentum gauge) is -24.08, below its signal line at -21.03, and the histogram is -3.05, so selling pressure is still building. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 28.51, so the stock is stretched to the downside. Price closed just under the lower Bollinger Band at $82.06 (the bands are a 20-day average ±2 standard deviations). The bands are very wide, with the upper band at $186.9, which signals extreme volatility. Volume was 49.5M, about 1.6x the 20-day average of 31.2M, which confirms the selling. The 52-week low of $73.52 is the nearest support, and the $93–94 zone from Friday\u0027s open and high is nearby resistance.",
               "fundamental":  "Nebius rents out GPU computing power and AI software tools, sitting between Nvidia\u0027s chips and the companies that build AI models. In Q3 2026 (reported 2026-11-12), revenue was $980.8M, up 571% year on year, though about $325M of that was one-time. Adjusted EBITDA was $465.8M, and contracted backlog (RPO) reached $38.7B. Nebius has signed more than $12B of contracts since the start of Q3, with customers including Microsoft, Meta, Databricks and Palantir. Demand is not the problem; delivery and funding are. Third-party data-center capacity slipped into 1H 2027. As a result, 2026 revenue is guided to the low end of $3.0–3.4B, and the target of more than $7B ARR (annualized revenue run rate) moved from December to February. Capex rises to about $27B in 2026 and $40–45B in 2027. Against that, Nebius has $7.4B in cash and $14.2B in debt, and it does not expect pre-tax breakeven until Q4 2027.",
               "news":  [
                            "2026-11-12: Q3 revenue of $980.8M (+571% YoY) and adjusted EBITDA of $465.8M beat estimates. However, Nebius guided 2026 revenue to the low end of $3.0–3.4B and pushed its \u003e$7B ARR goal from December to February. The stock fell 16.35% that day to $129.40.",
                            "2026-11-12: Nebius raised 2026 capex guidance to about $27B from $20–25B. Its first 2027 outlook calls for $12–13B in revenue and $40–45B in capex, with 75–85% to be covered by customer prepayments and asset-backed financing.",
                            "CEO Arkady Volozh wrote that \u0027where we rely on third parties, there have been delays.\u0027 The remaining Microsoft-contracted tranches at the New Jersey colocation site move to Q2 2027 while that site switches power to Bloom Energy fuel cells. He said Nebius-owned sites are on time or ahead of schedule.",
                            "The Q3 shareholder letter describes $5.75B of convertible notes issued in August, about $1.0B raised through at-the-market share sales in Q3, and a $3.5B senior secured facility with Citi in October. These show how heavily the build-out relies on outside capital.",
                            "Supply chain: on 2026-11-12, Nvidia announced that Jensen Huang had resigned as CEO, effective immediately, and named CFO Colette Kress interim CEO. Leadership uncertainty at Nebius\u0027s key GPU supplier weighed on AI infrastructure stocks going into Nvidia\u0027s earnings the following week.",
                            "2026-11-20: the neocloud selloff deepened as the rally around Anthropic\u0027s IPO faded and the Nasdaq fell 1.8%. Michael Burry, who had disclosed short positions in Nebius, publicly claimed vindication. Bernstein and Evercore ISI said capacity and financing, not customer demand, are the sector\u0027s binding constraints."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The bear case is about money and timing. Nebius must fund about $27B of capex this year and $40–45B next year. Meanwhile, Q3 operating cash flow was -$273M and debt is already $14.2B. More convertible notes or share sales would dilute existing holders, and tighter credit would raise funding costs. Delivery also depends partly on third-party data centers that have already slipped. On the chart there is no sign of a bottom yet: MACD is still widening lower, and a break below $73.52 would mean new 52-week lows.",
               "opportunity":  "The bull case is that demand looks real. Nebius has $38.7B of contracted backlog, signed more than $12B of contracts in about four months, has blue-chip customers such as Microsoft and Meta, and kept its ~40% adjusted EBITDA margin guidance. Management says the delays are about timing, not cancellations, and its own sites are on schedule. If the delayed capacity arrives in 1H 2027, the $12–13B revenue outlook for 2027 would be roughly 4x 2026. With RSI at 28.51 and the price below the lower band, the stock is deeply oversold, which can come before sharp relief rallies. However, oversold stocks can also keep falling.",
               "risks":  [
                             "Financing risk: $40–45B of 2027 capex depends on customer prepayments, asset-backed loans and capital markets staying open.",
                             "Dilution from further convertible notes or at-the-market share sales.",
                             "Further delays at third-party colocation sites, including the Microsoft New Jersey tranches, could push revenue later again.",
                             "Customer concentration in a few hyperscalers (Microsoft, Meta).",
                             "Nvidia leadership uncertainty and the broader AI-infrastructure sentiment shift, including prominent short-sellers targeting neoclouds.",
                             "Earnings quality: about $325M of Q3 revenue was non-recurring, and management changed its key metric away from ARR."
                         ],
               "summary":  "NBIS has fallen from $154.69 before its 2026-11-12 earnings report to $81.31 on 2026-11-20, a drop of about 47%, and now trades only about 11% above its 52-week low. Q3 beat estimates, but delayed third-party capacity, a pushed-back ARR target and a much bigger capex bill shifted the debate from growth to whether Nebius can fund and deliver it. Nvidia\u0027s surprise CEO exit and a sector-wide neocloud selloff added pressure. The technicals are firmly bearish, though oversold. Demand looks intact, but the thesis is on watch for execution and financing."
